title: The object is the visible result of a way of working
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

The [[Oka Chair]] has chunky legs because the wood is salvaged city timber, full of knots and worm holes. The parts were made wide enough that no one has to wonder whether a knot is holding the chair up.

So you can read the method off the chair. The legs tell you where the wood came from.

Other objects that show how they were made:

* [[Sedia 1]] by [[Enzo Mari]] is boards and nails, because the point was that you build it yourself.
* [[Solar.LowTechMagazine.com]] has dithered images and a battery meter, because it runs on one small solar panel.
* The [[Catifa Carta]] is black because that is how the shell comes out of the press.
* The [[Wishbone Chair]] is wood and paper cord. Was that a method, or just how chairs were made?

It runs the other way too. A chair with no trace of how it was made is also telling you something.

This is [[Design as an Attitude]] seen from the object's side.

See [[Truth to Materials]], [[Process]], [[Invest in the Process]], [[Form <> Content <> Context]], [[Doing Design is Important; Being a Designer is not.]]
